# 9. CORE DIFFERENTIATOR: RISKTRUST RELIABILITY ENGINE

Build a dedicated reliability-auditing service.

It must evaluate several independent signals rather than combining everything into an unexplained arbitrary score.

## A. Calibration reliability
- Evaluate whether predicted probabilities agree with observed frequencies.
- Display Brier score and calibration plots.
- Compare calibration across model versions.
- Document calibration limitations.

## B. Input reliability
- Detect missing or invalid fields.
- Compare inputs against the training distribution.
- Identify unsupported feature combinations.
- Flag possible out-of-distribution observations.
- Explain why the system may not be able to provide a reliable estimate.

## C. Model disagreement
- Compare independently trained models on the same supported input.
- Display disagreement where models disagree.
- Explain that disagreement is a warning signal, not proof that the prediction is wrong.
- Avoid averaging incompatible scores without a defensible method.

## D. Explanation reliability
- Measure the stability of explanations under small, plausible input perturbations.
- Compare global feature rankings across training folds or model versions.
- Report changes in feature contributions.
- Identify explanations that are sensitive to perturbations.
- Do not present correlated features as independent causes.

## E. Counterfactual reliability
- Validate generated counterfactuals.
- Confirm that the model output actually changes as claimed.
- Identify implausible or infeasible changes.
- Report whether a valid counterfactual could be found.
- Never imply that changing a feature guarantees a health outcome.

Display these components separately in the interface. Do not create an arbitrary overall reliability score unless its formula is documented and empirically evaluated.

